1
01:00:00,099 --> 01:00:01,933
<b>This is the most important thing written</b>

2
01:00:01,933 --> 01:00:03,333
<b>on the Piri Reis map.</b>

3
01:00:03,599 --> 01:00:05,300
<b>A paragraph in Ottoman</b>

4
01:00:05,333 --> 01:00:07,433
<b>Turkish tucked in the corner.</b>

5
01:00:08,066 --> 01:00:09,933
<b>You've probably seen this map. People</b>

6
01:00:09,933 --> 01:00:10,933
<b>have called it the most</b>

7
01:00:10,933 --> 01:00:12,966
<b>accurate map of the 16th century.</b>

8
01:00:13,266 --> 01:00:16,466
<b>Proof of its seafaring civilization, tens</b>

9
01:00:16,466 --> 01:00:17,900
<b>of thousands of years old.</b>

10
01:00:18,199 --> 01:00:21,133
<b>Proof the earth was visited by aliens.</b>

11
01:00:21,433 --> 01:00:23,833
<b>It's the one document the whole lost</b>

12
01:00:23,833 --> 01:00:26,199
<b>civilization case keeps coming back to.</b>

13
01:00:40,599 --> 01:00:42,699
<b>This video is about one document and what</b>

14
01:00:42,699 --> 01:00:44,833
<b>it says when you actually read it. The</b>

15
01:00:44,833 --> 01:00:46,166
<b>answers are on the map.</b>

16
01:00:46,366 --> 01:00:47,666
<b>People just don't read it.</b>

17
01:00:48,333 --> 01:00:50,300
<b>Starting the corner, the most important</b>

18
01:00:50,300 --> 01:00:52,333
<b>thing on this map is the paragraph there.</b>

19
01:00:52,666 --> 01:00:54,933
<b>Historians number it in description 6.</b>

20
01:00:55,466 --> 01:00:57,099
<b>I'm going to call it a source list</b>

21
01:00:57,133 --> 01:00:58,900
<b>because that's what it is.</b>

22
01:00:59,366 --> 01:01:00,933
<b>I have used 20 maps and</b>

23
01:01:00,933 --> 01:01:03,300
<b>Mapamundi, 8 such cafegares,</b>

24
01:01:03,966 --> 01:01:06,400
<b>one of the Arab maps of India, four maps</b>

25
01:01:06,400 --> 01:01:08,066
<b>made by the Portuguese and</b>

26
01:01:08,066 --> 01:01:10,300
<b>finally a map drawn by Columbus.</b>

27
01:01:11,133 --> 01:01:13,900
<b>Pirioureis says it himself. He took other</b>

28
01:01:13,900 --> 01:01:15,033
<b>people's maps and</b>

29
01:01:15,033 --> 01:01:16,599
<b>brought them to one scale.</b>

30
01:01:16,866 --> 01:01:19,366
<b>He didn't find this map. He put it</b>

31
01:01:19,366 --> 01:01:20,733
<b>together from other maps.</b>

32
01:01:21,500 --> 01:01:23,800
<b>Here's Hancock's claim. The maps</b>

33
01:01:23,800 --> 01:01:26,699
<b>Pirioureis copied weren't ordinary maps.</b>

34
01:01:26,833 --> 01:01:28,400
<b>They were survivals from a lost</b>

35
01:01:28,400 --> 01:01:30,833
<b>civilization preserved for thousands of</b>

36
01:01:30,833 --> 01:01:32,699
<b>years in the library of Alexandria.</b>

37
01:01:33,433 --> 01:01:36,533
<b>But Pirioureis names his sources and none</b>

38
01:01:36,533 --> 01:01:37,733
<b>of them are ancient.</b>

39
01:01:38,199 --> 01:01:40,800
<b>Eight Ptolemaic textbook maps, four</b>

40
01:01:40,800 --> 01:01:43,566
<b>recent Portuguese charts, a map drawn by</b>

41
01:01:43,566 --> 01:01:45,433
<b>Columbus within living memory.</b>

42
01:01:46,300 --> 01:01:48,099
<b>Hancock got the library of Alexandria</b>

43
01:01:48,099 --> 01:01:49,733
<b>from Charles Hapgood in</b>

44
01:01:50,099 --> 01:01:53,266
<b>1966 and Hapgood got it in</b>

45
01:01:53,266 --> 01:01:55,000
<b>part from the map itself.</b>

46
01:01:55,666 --> 01:01:57,433
<b>The source list really does say the</b>

47
01:01:57,433 --> 01:01:59,199
<b>oldest charts go back to the time of</b>

48
01:01:59,199 --> 01:02:00,433
<b>Alexander the Great.</b>

49
01:02:00,966 --> 01:02:02,166
<b>But that last step is</b>

50
01:02:02,166 --> 01:02:04,433
<b>his, not the primary source.</b>

51
01:02:05,533 --> 01:02:07,833
<b>Mäkingdös, who wrote the authoritative</b>

52
01:02:07,833 --> 01:02:09,833
<b>book on this map, checked the map and</b>

53
01:02:09,833 --> 01:02:12,066
<b>everything else Pirioureis wrote and</b>

54
01:02:12,099 --> 01:02:14,500
<b>the claim simply isn't there. And there's</b>

55
01:02:14,500 --> 01:02:15,466
<b>one word in the same</b>

56
01:02:15,466 --> 01:02:16,800
<b>line worth stopping on.</b>

57
01:02:17,366 --> 01:02:19,366
<b>Pirioureis says those eight maps go back</b>

58
01:02:19,366 --> 01:02:20,666
<b>to the time of Alexander</b>

59
01:02:20,966 --> 01:02:22,233
<b>and he gives them a name.</b>

60
01:02:22,833 --> 01:02:23,333
<b>"Cafiria?"</b>

61
01:02:24,033 --> 01:02:25,900
<b>Sounds like it should mean something. The</b>

62
01:02:25,900 --> 01:02:27,566
<b>title of some old</b>

63
01:02:27,566 --> 01:02:29,033
<b>mysterious class of a map.</b>

64
01:02:29,466 --> 01:02:30,233
<b>Old enough to carry</b>

65
01:02:30,233 --> 01:02:32,433
<b>lost knowledge. It isn't.</b>

66
01:02:33,866 --> 01:02:35,800
<b>Every scholar who can read the original</b>

67
01:02:35,833 --> 01:02:36,599
<b>reaches the same</b>

68
01:02:36,599 --> 01:02:39,400
<b>conclusion. It's a spelling mistake.</b>

69
01:02:40,300 --> 01:02:41,333
<b>"Cafiria" is the</b>

70
01:02:41,333 --> 01:02:43,000
<b>Arabic word for geography.</b>

71
01:02:43,666 --> 01:02:45,633
<b>"Tughrafia" spelled wrong.</b>

72
01:02:46,166 --> 01:02:48,900
<b>In Arabic the letter for the "gh" sound</b>

73
01:02:49,066 --> 01:02:50,433
<b>is identical to another</b>

74
01:02:50,466 --> 01:02:52,233
<b>except for one dot on the top.</b>

75
01:02:52,766 --> 01:02:55,433
<b>Drop the dot, swap one more letter and</b>

76
01:02:55,833 --> 01:02:57,800
<b>"Tughrafia" turns into "Kafirgia".</b>

77
01:02:58,733 --> 01:03:00,233
<b>A word that means nothing.</b>

78
01:03:00,933 --> 01:03:02,766
<b>Those eight "Kafirias"</b>

79
01:03:02,766 --> 01:03:04,666
<b>were ordinary geography maps.</b>

80
01:03:05,866 --> 01:03:07,833
<b>Copies of Claudius Petolomeis Atlas</b>

81
01:03:07,833 --> 01:03:10,033
<b>reprinted all over Renaissance Europe.</b>

82
01:03:10,766 --> 01:03:12,066
<b>Nothing ancient about them.</b>

83
01:03:12,933 --> 01:03:14,599
<b>People call them ancient anyway because</b>

84
01:03:14,599 --> 01:03:17,300
<b>of one more mix-up. The same one that put</b>

85
01:03:17,300 --> 01:03:18,733
<b>Alexander's name on the map.</b>

86
01:03:19,466 --> 01:03:21,699
<b>There are two famous Petolomeis and they</b>

87
01:03:21,699 --> 01:03:23,400
<b>lived 300 years apart.</b>

88
01:03:23,933 --> 01:03:26,400
<b>Claudius Petolomeis, the map maker,</b>

89
01:03:26,666 --> 01:03:28,599
<b>worked around 150 AD.</b>

90
01:03:29,566 --> 01:03:31,366
<b>Petolomei I was one of</b>

91
01:03:31,366 --> 01:03:32,733
<b>Alexander the Great's generals.</b>

92
01:03:33,866 --> 01:03:36,199
<b>P.D. Reyes confused the two. He thought</b>

93
01:03:36,199 --> 01:03:37,866
<b>his maps reached all the way back to</b>

94
01:03:37,866 --> 01:03:40,233
<b>Petolomei I in Alexander's day.</b>

95
01:03:41,033 --> 01:03:43,033
<b>They didn't, but that one slip is where</b>

96
01:03:43,033 --> 01:03:44,666
<b>the time of Alexander on the source list</b>

97
01:03:44,666 --> 01:03:46,433
<b>comes from and it's the line</b>

98
01:03:46,699 --> 01:03:48,433
<b>Hapgood built his library on.</b>

99
01:03:49,199 --> 01:03:50,400
<b>And none of this is new.</b>

100
01:03:51,133 --> 01:03:52,566
<b>The first skull to make sense of this</b>

101
01:03:52,566 --> 01:03:55,599
<b>map, Paul Calle, worked it out in 1933.</b>

102
01:03:56,500 --> 01:03:58,000
<b>"Giafaria" is a mistake</b>

103
01:03:58,000 --> 01:04:00,233
<b>for "Tughrafia" geography.</b>

104
01:04:01,166 --> 01:04:02,633
<b>That tells us where the sources came</b>

105
01:04:02,633 --> 01:04:04,699
<b>from. Now let's look at the map itself.</b>

106
01:04:05,599 --> 01:04:07,133
<b>The Caribbean section is the one Hapgood</b>

107
01:04:07,133 --> 01:04:09,766
<b>points to the most often. In 2019,</b>

108
01:04:10,233 --> 01:04:13,233
<b>he said this island east of Florida shows</b>

109
01:04:13,233 --> 01:04:14,766
<b>the Bimini Road, a line of</b>

110
01:04:14,766 --> 01:04:16,500
<b>stone blocks of the Bahamas,</b>

111
01:04:16,966 --> 01:04:18,166
<b>drawn while they were still</b>

112
01:04:18,166 --> 01:04:20,000
<b>above water in their Ice Age.</b>

113
01:04:20,733 --> 01:04:23,233
<b>P.D. Reyes labeled that ghost himself.</b>

114
01:04:23,699 --> 01:04:26,166
<b>Right where Hankook points, the island he</b>

115
01:04:26,166 --> 01:04:27,266
<b>reads as the Bahamas,</b>

116
01:04:27,800 --> 01:04:29,266
<b>there are two place names.</b>

117
01:04:29,733 --> 01:04:32,599
<b>Porta Gande and Kao Punta Orafai.</b>

118
01:04:33,300 --> 01:04:34,533
<b>Both are Columbus's</b>

119
01:04:34,833 --> 01:04:36,199
<b>from his second voyage.</b>

120
01:04:36,699 --> 01:04:38,166
<b>Macintosh checked every</b>

121
01:04:38,166 --> 01:04:40,000
<b>surviving charge from the period.</b>

122
01:04:40,433 --> 01:04:41,633
<b>These two names appear</b>

123
01:04:41,633 --> 01:04:43,233
<b>on only one map on Earth.</b>

124
01:04:43,800 --> 01:04:44,366
<b>This one.</b>

125
01:04:45,000 --> 01:04:46,800
<b>Which puts the spot Hanka calls the</b>

126
01:04:46,800 --> 01:04:49,933
<b>Bimini Road in Cuba, not the Bahamas.</b>

127
01:04:50,566 --> 01:04:52,900
<b>Copied, name for name, from a map by</b>

128
01:04:52,900 --> 01:04:54,199
<b>Christopher Columbus.</b>

129
01:04:54,766 --> 01:04:56,933
<b>Not 12,000 years ago, the</b>

130
01:04:56,933 --> 01:04:59,233
<b>work of a man who died in 1506.</b>

131
01:04:59,933 --> 01:05:01,833
<b>There is one small mystery here.</b>

132
01:05:02,266 --> 01:05:04,000
<b>How does an Ottoman admiral</b>

133
01:05:04,066 --> 01:05:05,933
<b>end up with a map by Columbus?</b>

134
01:05:06,800 --> 01:05:08,599
<b>The inscription says it came of a Spanish</b>

135
01:05:08,599 --> 01:05:11,033
<b>prisoner captured by P.D. Reyes' uncle,</b>

136
01:05:11,166 --> 01:05:12,233
<b>who claimed he'd sailed</b>

137
01:05:12,233 --> 01:05:13,900
<b>with Columbus three times.</b>

138
01:05:14,599 --> 01:05:17,099
<b>He may have exaggerated, but the place</b>

139
01:05:17,133 --> 01:05:18,566
<b>names hold either way.</b>

140
01:05:19,400 --> 01:05:20,833
<b>And there is one more detail that prints</b>

141
01:05:20,833 --> 01:05:22,066
<b>this section to Columbus's</b>

142
01:05:22,066 --> 01:05:24,300
<b>lifetime, not the Ice Age.</b>

143
01:05:24,800 --> 01:05:26,400
<b>Look at how Cuba is drawn.</b>

144
01:05:26,766 --> 01:05:29,833
<b>On the 1513 map, Cuba isn't an island.</b>

145
01:05:30,033 --> 01:05:31,699
<b>It's attached to the mainland.</b>

146
01:05:32,199 --> 01:05:33,766
<b>That's Columbus's mistake.</b>

147
01:05:34,099 --> 01:05:36,733
<b>He was so sure Cuba was the edge of Asia</b>

148
01:05:37,033 --> 01:05:39,333
<b>that on June 12th, 1494,</b>

149
01:05:39,800 --> 01:05:42,133
<b>he sent a notary from ship to ship to</b>

150
01:05:42,133 --> 01:05:43,900
<b>make his whole crew swear to it.</b>

151
01:05:44,233 --> 01:05:45,833
<b>Anyone who ever took it back</b>

152
01:05:45,833 --> 01:05:47,366
<b>would have his stone cut out.</b>

153
01:05:48,066 --> 01:05:50,199
<b>The map copies that error exactly.</b>

154
01:05:50,766 --> 01:05:52,400
<b>And you can watch it get corrected.</b>

155
01:05:53,033 --> 01:05:56,000
<b>P.D. Reyes made a second map in 1528,</b>

156
01:05:56,599 --> 01:05:58,099
<b>and by then ships had</b>

157
01:05:58,099 --> 01:05:59,666
<b>sailed all the way around Cuba.</b>

158
01:05:59,933 --> 01:06:03,199
<b>So on the 1528 map, Cuba is an island.</b>

159
01:06:03,599 --> 01:06:04,766
<b>The map kept talk with</b>

160
01:06:04,766 --> 01:06:06,366
<b>what explorers were learning.</b>

161
01:06:06,733 --> 01:06:08,900
<b>A 12,000-year-old map wouldn't carry</b>

162
01:06:08,900 --> 01:06:10,833
<b>Columbus's personal blunder</b>

163
01:06:11,066 --> 01:06:12,033
<b>and wouldn't quietly</b>

164
01:06:12,033 --> 01:06:13,966
<b>fix itself 15 years later.</b>

165
01:06:14,433 --> 01:06:17,099
<b>So the ancient maps weren't ancient.</b>

166
01:06:17,333 --> 01:06:19,566
<b>They were Columbus's first rough charts</b>

167
01:06:19,566 --> 01:06:22,333
<b>of the Caribbean from around 1495,</b>

168
01:06:23,266 --> 01:06:24,733
<b>and the originals are lost.</b>

169
01:06:24,866 --> 01:06:25,699
<b>Which means that this</b>

170
01:06:25,699 --> 01:06:26,866
<b>corner of an Ottoman map</b>

171
01:06:27,199 --> 01:06:29,199
<b>is the closest thing left on Earth to</b>

172
01:06:29,199 --> 01:06:31,466
<b>Columbus's own lost cartography.</b>

173
01:06:31,766 --> 01:06:33,766
<b>This is what I try to do on this channel.</b>

174
01:06:33,800 --> 01:06:35,699
<b>Go to the document and read it.</b>

175
01:06:35,966 --> 01:06:37,599
<b>If you like that, subscribe,</b>

176
01:06:38,099 --> 01:06:38,800
<b>because we haven't</b>

177
01:06:38,800 --> 01:06:40,166
<b>finished reading this one.</b>

178
01:06:40,400 --> 01:06:41,900
<b>They're still the bottom of the map.</b>

179
01:06:42,733 --> 01:06:44,300
<b>It's the part that Henco can</b>

180
01:06:44,300 --> 01:06:46,133
<b>have good readers Antarctica,</b>

181
01:06:46,400 --> 01:06:48,033
<b>mapped before the ice.</b>

182
01:06:48,599 --> 01:06:50,766
<b>Two coasts, drawn as one.</b>

183
01:06:51,033 --> 01:06:52,300
<b>The top is Brazil.</b>

184
01:06:52,566 --> 01:06:53,599
<b>Rio de Janeiro.</b>

185
01:06:54,133 --> 01:06:54,800
<b>Calanea.</b>

186
01:06:55,166 --> 01:06:56,366
<b>The Portuguese name's</b>

187
01:06:56,366 --> 01:06:58,199
<b>running south in the right order,</b>

188
01:06:58,333 --> 01:07:00,033
<b>right where they sit on a real map.</b>

189
01:07:00,633 --> 01:07:01,800
<b>The Portuguese sailed</b>

190
01:07:01,800 --> 01:07:03,199
<b>that coast and named it.</b>

191
01:07:03,766 --> 01:07:06,366
<b>Then it bends into a huge empty continent</b>

192
01:07:06,366 --> 01:07:07,800
<b>across the bottom of the map.</b>

193
01:07:08,633 --> 01:07:10,566
<b>That part isn't a discovery.</b>

194
01:07:11,133 --> 01:07:13,466
<b>Every map maker in the 1500s</b>

195
01:07:13,533 --> 01:07:14,766
<b>drew a giant southern</b>

196
01:07:14,766 --> 01:07:15,866
<b>landmass down there.</b>

197
01:07:16,133 --> 01:07:18,900
<b>One had to exist to balance the globe.</b>

198
01:07:19,466 --> 01:07:21,300
<b>And Piri ran the coast of</b>

199
01:07:21,333 --> 01:07:23,000
<b>Brazil straight into it.</b>

200
01:07:23,800 --> 01:07:25,133
<b>That invented continent is</b>

201
01:07:25,133 --> 01:07:26,866
<b>what Hapgood calls Antarctica.</b>

202
01:07:27,733 --> 01:07:29,599
<b>And it's where we find Excription 10.</b>

203
01:07:30,699 --> 01:07:31,766
<b>This country is barren.</b>

204
01:07:32,300 --> 01:07:33,366
<b>Everything is desolate.</b>

205
01:07:34,366 --> 01:07:36,133
<b>Large serpents are found here.</b>

206
01:07:36,666 --> 01:07:37,966
<b>The Portuguese infidels</b>

207
01:07:37,966 --> 01:07:39,633
<b>did not land on these shores.</b>

208
01:07:40,300 --> 01:07:41,199
<b>These shores are also</b>

209
01:07:41,199 --> 01:07:42,733
<b>said to be very hot.</b>

210
01:07:43,400 --> 01:07:44,433
<b>The Portuguese named</b>

211
01:07:44,433 --> 01:07:45,766
<b>the coast they sailed.</b>

212
01:07:46,133 --> 01:07:46,900
<b>Brazil.</b>

213
01:07:47,300 --> 01:07:48,433
<b>They never landed on the</b>

214
01:07:48,433 --> 01:07:49,699
<b>empty stretch below it,</b>

215
01:07:50,000 --> 01:07:52,099
<b>because there was barely anything there.</b>

216
01:07:52,633 --> 01:07:53,966
<b>Hot, barren,</b>

217
01:07:54,233 --> 01:07:56,833
<b>half-invented, not an icecap.</b>

218
01:07:57,633 --> 01:07:58,866
<b>And Piri Reis wasn't</b>

219
01:07:58,866 --> 01:08:00,300
<b>the only map to do this.</b>

220
01:08:00,633 --> 01:08:01,533
<b>They all did.</b>

221
01:08:02,099 --> 01:08:03,266
<b>That's the giveaway.</b>

222
01:08:03,833 --> 01:08:05,466
<b>No two of them drew the southern</b>

223
01:08:05,466 --> 01:08:06,800
<b>continent the same way,</b>

224
01:08:06,933 --> 01:08:07,699
<b>because none of them</b>

225
01:08:07,699 --> 01:08:09,000
<b>had actually seen it.</b>

226
01:08:09,833 --> 01:08:11,633
<b>So the lost civilization writers</b>

227
01:08:12,033 --> 01:08:14,033
<b>kept a handful whose guesses happened to</b>

228
01:08:14,033 --> 01:08:15,266
<b>resemble Antarctica.</b>

229
01:08:16,033 --> 01:08:17,666
<b>Piri Reis among them, and</b>

230
01:08:17,699 --> 01:08:19,433
<b>ignored the dozens that didn't.</b>

231
01:08:20,066 --> 01:08:22,100
<b>Real maps of a real coast</b>

232
01:08:22,300 --> 01:08:23,500
<b>would agree with each other.</b>

233
01:08:24,133 --> 01:08:25,233
<b>These don't.</b>

234
01:08:25,733 --> 01:08:27,066
<b>That isn't evidence.</b>

235
01:08:27,199 --> 01:08:28,766
<b>It's shopping for it.</b>

236
01:08:29,399 --> 01:08:31,466
<b>This shape was never the real story.</b>

237
01:08:31,666 --> 01:08:33,500
<b>This map sat forgotten in a palace</b>

238
01:08:33,500 --> 01:08:35,666
<b>library for 400 years.</b>

239
01:08:35,966 --> 01:08:36,766
<b>Not hidden.</b>

240
01:08:37,066 --> 01:08:38,133
<b>Not suppressed.</b>

241
01:08:38,500 --> 01:08:39,866
<b>Just ignored.</b>

242
01:08:40,500 --> 01:08:41,600
<b>And being ignored is the</b>

243
01:08:41,600 --> 01:08:43,000
<b>only thing that saved it.</b>

244
01:08:43,300 --> 01:08:45,533
<b>Piri Reis was drawing this map for an</b>

245
01:08:45,533 --> 01:08:46,933
<b>empire whose government,</b>

246
01:08:47,300 --> 01:08:50,500
<b>the Porte, the Sultan, and his court had</b>

247
01:08:50,500 --> 01:08:51,933
<b>no use for what he did.</b>

248
01:08:52,399 --> 01:08:54,800
<b>In the same century, Spain and Portugal</b>

249
01:08:54,866 --> 01:08:56,466
<b>each ran a government office</b>

250
01:08:56,466 --> 01:08:58,866
<b>where each voyage's findings got added to</b>

251
01:08:58,866 --> 01:09:00,199
<b>a single master chart,</b>

252
01:09:00,533 --> 01:09:02,800
<b>kept up to date by paid cartographers.</b>

253
01:09:03,433 --> 01:09:04,800
<b>The Ottoman state had nothing</b>

254
01:09:04,800 --> 01:09:07,399
<b>like that, and didn't want it.</b>

255
01:09:07,899 --> 01:09:09,566
<b>As Svaat Süzegh puts it,</b>

256
01:09:09,966 --> 01:09:12,633
<b>"Piri Reis's maps represented a world</b>

257
01:09:12,666 --> 01:09:13,633
<b>that lay beyond the</b>

258
01:09:13,633 --> 01:09:15,300
<b>horizon of the Porte's ambition.</b>

259
01:09:16,199 --> 01:09:18,366
<b>The map was rolled up and shelved."</b>

260
01:09:19,033 --> 01:09:22,699
<b>As for Piri Reis, around 1553, in his</b>

261
01:09:22,699 --> 01:09:24,933
<b>80s, he lost a sea battle.</b>

262
01:09:25,166 --> 01:09:26,399
<b>The governor of Egypt</b>

263
01:09:26,433 --> 01:09:27,933
<b>reported him to the Sultan.</b>

264
01:09:28,233 --> 01:09:30,566
<b>He died never knowing that the map he'd</b>

265
01:09:30,600 --> 01:09:31,899
<b>drawn was still</b>

266
01:09:31,899 --> 01:09:33,433
<b>sitting where he'd left it.</b>

267
01:09:34,266 --> 01:09:35,333
<b>Süzegh calls it,</b>

268
01:09:35,733 --> 01:09:38,433
<b>"a strange map, used mutilated remains</b>

269
01:09:38,433 --> 01:09:39,300
<b>lay forgotten in the</b>

270
01:09:39,300 --> 01:09:41,166
<b>library of Tokapi Palace."</b>

271
01:09:41,966 --> 01:09:43,866
<b>And there it stayed, in a storeroom</b>

272
01:09:43,866 --> 01:09:44,733
<b>nobody had properly</b>

273
01:09:44,733 --> 01:09:46,500
<b>catalogued until 1929,</b>

274
01:09:47,033 --> 01:09:48,833
<b>when the palace was being turned into a</b>

275
01:09:48,833 --> 01:09:51,033
<b>museum and the director found it.</b>

276
01:09:51,666 --> 01:09:55,333
<b>It reached Paul Kalle, who, by luck, had</b>

277
01:09:55,333 --> 01:09:56,500
<b>spent the previous years</b>

278
01:09:56,500 --> 01:09:57,966
<b>on Piri Reis' other book,</b>

279
01:09:58,233 --> 01:09:59,300
<b>the Sailing Manual.</b>

280
01:09:59,733 --> 01:10:01,699
<b>He knew the hand the moment he saw it,</b>

281
01:10:01,933 --> 01:10:03,266
<b>and he caught what no</b>

282
01:10:03,266 --> 01:10:05,033
<b>one had in four centuries.</b>

283
01:10:05,600 --> 01:10:08,100
<b>The map says, in writing, that it was</b>

284
01:10:08,100 --> 01:10:09,399
<b>partially copied from</b>

285
01:10:09,399 --> 01:10:10,766
<b>a chart by Columbus.</b>

286
01:10:11,466 --> 01:10:12,633
<b>So what does the Piri</b>

287
01:10:12,633 --> 01:10:14,233
<b>Reis map actually say?</b>

288
01:10:14,699 --> 01:10:16,866
<b>It tells you where every line on it came</b>

289
01:10:16,866 --> 01:10:18,399
<b>from, and what every</b>

290
01:10:18,399 --> 01:10:20,033
<b>piece of land really is.</b>

291
01:10:20,666 --> 01:10:22,100
<b>Piri Reis wrote it down</b>

292
01:10:22,100 --> 01:10:25,300
<b>himself in 1513 on the parchment.</b>

293
01:10:25,733 --> 01:10:27,066
<b>We just didn't read it.</b>

